<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=celluloid">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Celluloid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Parkesine, xylonite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Celluloid is a class of plastics made by mixing nitrocellulose and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>. Alexander Parkes made the first celluloid as a bulk material for forming objects in 1855 in Birmingham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and patented it as Parkesine in 1862, showing it at the International Exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that year. The American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wesley">John Wesley</a> Hyatt acquired Parkes's patent, and in 1870 he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> patented a process using cellulose nitrate and camphor as a plasticizer; Isaiah named it celluloid in 1872. It was used for billiard balls and as a cheaper substitute for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and until the 1950s it was the base of most photographic and motion-picture film.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/celluloid.jpg" alt="Celluloid" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcel Oosterwijk from Amsterdam, The Netherlands · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_celluloid_film_rolls_(5201105455).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1855</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Birmingham, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Alexander Parkes · John Wesley Hyatt · Daniel Spill</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Daniel Spill, who took over Parkes's patents as the Xylonite Co., disputed the Hyatts' claims in court cases from 1877 to 1884; the judge ruled that neither party held an exclusive claim and that the true inventor was Parkes. Collodion, invented in 1846, dries to a similar film.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cinematograph">Cinematograph<span>1892</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=celluloid">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
